#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198151 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f0da82-f79b-5056-ac99-b401eb1630c7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "06ee94f5-8d8c-537b-83ff-69d6e351e4d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13145" :question/problem "![](images/89/89fa15ffb9a7f6b319cf3b9ff0de4ab821a15f632d7f93618fbe99b7979260f4.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation." :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{ -3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{ (x,y)  \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2}\n\\: : \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}  \n\\leq  x \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3} , \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}  \n\\leq  y \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \n\\Big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198152 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198476 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions"}] :question/id #uuid "487a42f5-a562-5df1-9225-dbbc0046c002" :question/math-academy-id "e-13156" :question/problem "![](images/45/45260087c5196cc239f21402d73169f60139bd7d2c10ee0aa1db4e1a2c56be4a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2 } \\: : \\:  2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt } y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3 \\Big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198153 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198477 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "88fba9fd-d05b-546c-89aa-edcf8d2fc93e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13158" :question/problem "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f5f2f2e67ba0ad566b2759ca5536d248f671bf0f082d567a7f7d993772601e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\gt 400.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 }x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25 }y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400 \\bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198163 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e694b201-5ec6-5cc3-a608-2447d1cc1ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, One Condition"}] :question/id #uuid "56561d78-d192-5197-81f7-303abf75018f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13183" :question/problem "![](images/78/787b62fbe367feae9e4c8ed10b983a256840e3a93727fea7c1ff67cacad0129d.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x+2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\: : \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f0da82-f79b-5056-ac99-b401eb1630c7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde48367-e769-503e-8ad6-0c5d9f897ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374396} {:answer/id #uuid "c7894e2b-1060-5e26-8a29-0c4f787e571e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7894e2b-1060-5e26-8a29-0c4f787e571e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374397} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83530ca-fbbe-51cb-a26a-450659c0e6a5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374395} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6907ae38-4cab-542f-aec0-edb74aec88f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374399} {:answer/id #uuid "8d88ff4a-3ee2-549e-ba1c-d5c20bba3069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d88ff4a-3ee2-549e-ba1c-d5c20bba3069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a89cf05b-dcae-59f6-a446-92398496ca87" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0237cc93-9a15-541a-9d00-3189dee5eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374402} {:answer/id #uuid "174aa03e-49b1-5db3-82aa-38ce30dc1411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0237cc93-9a15-541a-9d00-3189dee5eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374402} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3157b98-0c9b-5429-ad6c-5f32fc31031f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d92890b-6106-515d-8c3c-b939fdacd758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374405} {:answer/id #uuid "d04e5716-9d6a-5f97-8a55-69c57553d296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d92890b-6106-515d-8c3c-b939fdacd758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374405} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b1e6a44-df3e-53cc-97e9-1b76ca3abff0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d62b961-39d3-5633-b650-62ddc2233d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181159" :question/problem "![](images/99/993cfa437e54f390237b85f3d7ee8a76b626d65431908fe039e0046ba0a33bb1.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\,1<y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to\n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=3$), and\n\n- *include* the bottom edge ($y=1$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\le x<6,\\,1\\le y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le x<6,\\,1\\le y<4\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198476 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1444e982-55d7-52e3-a040-5a1751634fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284966} {:answer/id #uuid "60551458-909d-586f-ad4c-d71329d3d8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285670} {:answer/id #uuid "745fcbcf-05c3-55e8-9741-493d7d05088a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285866} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8812c4-4f01-59a5-b4f9-a2f50f7403cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285937} {:answer/id #uuid "93bf3d77-ccf6-55db-90a7-83c37f0205f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1444e982-55d7-52e3-a040-5a1751634fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.t